WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.401
The open–closed principle encourages stable code
around deliberate extension points.

00:00:05.401 --> 00:00:11.573
In the shape example, a common drawing operation
keeps the painter independent of concrete shape

00:00:11.573 --> 00:00:11.958
types.

00:00:11.958 --> 00:00:16.330
This does not eliminate all edits or require
inheritance everywhere.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:22.760
Judge the abstraction by the changes it localizes
and the contract new implementations must satisfy.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.769
Liskov substitution preserves the expectations
clients are entitled to make.

00:00:27.769 --> 00:00:33.784
The mutable Rectangle and Square example fails
because setters violate independent-dimension

00:00:33.784 --> 00:00:34.372
behavior.

00:00:34.372 --> 00:00:37.707
Check actual preconditions, results and invariants.

00:00:37.707 --> 00:00:42.937
Exceptions are not automatically violations:
optional operations may allow them.

00:00:42.937 --> 00:00:48.560
The right question is whether the subtype honors the
contract the client was promised.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:55.110
Design interfaces around client roles and put high-
level policy behind appropriate abstractions.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:59.992
Read-only and mutable capabilities need not be
bundled for every client.

00:00:59.992 --> 00:01:05.348
JDBC shows how a stable API can separate application
logic from driver details.

00:01:05.348 --> 00:01:11.994
These boundaries reduce coupling, but only a
preserved behavioral contract makes replacement
safe.
